# AsyncCodeBench

AsyncCodeBench is a benchmark and construction pipeline for evaluating
asynchronous multi-agent software-engineering agents on real coding tasks.

The project studies a specific failure mode that standard final-pass coding
benchmarks usually hide: agents working concurrently from private, stale local
state while teammates' patches, test results, messages, and integration work
are still in flight.

The current active specification is:

```text
SPECIFICATION_v0.3.md
```

If this README conflicts with `SPECIFICATION_v0.3.md`, the specification takes
precedence.

## What This Project Is

AsyncCodeBench is not a synthetic task set and it is not just a collection of
multi-agent prompts. The v0.3 project turns public coding benchmark tasks into
dependency-aware asynchronous benchmark instances.

The core transformation is:

```text
public coding task
-> natural subproblem identification
-> producer/consumer dependency labeling
-> async scenario recomposition
-> dependency-level metrics
-> automatic validation gates
-> independent human annotation and adjudication
```

The current v0.3 data construction focuses on Commit0-derived tasks. Each
qualified task keeps the public source and public tests as the substrate, then
adds AsyncCodeBench metadata:

- task record;
- scenario record;
- quality record;
- async metrics manifest;
- human annotation forms;
- optional checksum-pinned bootstrap overlays;
- contract tests that validate the generated records.

The generated records are candidates for human review. They are not
`release_ready` just because the machine checks pass.

## How It Differs From Prior Work

Most repository-level coding benchmarks evaluate whether an agent can produce a
correct final patch. Most multi-agent benchmarks evaluate collaboration in
simulated, conversational, general, or embodied environments.

AsyncCodeBench targets the intersection:

```text
real repository-level coding tasks
+
asynchronous LLM software agents
+
private workspaces and controlled information visibility
+
public-test-observable dependency contracts
```

The main novelty is not "using multiple agents." It is making asynchrony itself
observable in real coding tasks. Async agents do not share a single up-to-date
workspace or a synchronized view of tests, messages, and teammate edits. One
agent can keep implementing against an assumption that was already invalidated
by another agent's hidden or delayed work.

AsyncCodeBench therefore asks whether teams can resolve dependency contracts
under delayed visibility and stale local state. The concrete contracts include:

- parser output shapes consumed by high-level APIs;
- schema or registry contracts consumed by nested behavior;
- utility parsing and normalization contracts consumed by core logic;
- shared state or type contracts that downstream agents may assume incorrectly.

This lets us measure failures caused by asynchronous execution, not just final
wrong answers: stale assumptions, late discovery of contract changes, delayed
integration, duplicated work, missing or ineffective communication, and
downstream code written against an outdated upstream artifact. These failures
can occur even when patches merge textually.

## Current v0.3 Status

As of the current repository state:

- Official Commit0 v0.3 tasks: `18`
- Excluded candidates: `fabric`, `python-progressbar`
- Non-official scratch/stress candidate retained for audit: `chardet`
- Archived non-official artifacts:
  `archive/non_official/commit0_v0.3/`

Current Commit0 v0.3 qualification-ready task records:

```text
commit0:cachetools
commit0:cookiecutter
commit0:deprecated
commit0:dulwich
commit0:fastapi
commit0:filesystem_spec
commit0:flask
commit0:graphene
commit0:imapclient
commit0:marshmallow
commit0:parsel
commit0:pexpect
commit0:portalocker
commit0:python-rsa
commit0:requests
commit0:simpy
commit0:tinydb
commit0:wcwidth
```

The current excluded or non-official candidates are:

```text
commit0:fabric
commit0:python-progressbar
commit0:chardet
```

The official 18-task list is the current experimental set unless and until the
human annotation and release rules mark a task otherwise. Historical artifacts
for excluded/non-official candidates are kept under
`archive/non_official/commit0_v0.3/` so prior construction and pilot runs are
auditable, but they are not part of official v0.3 results.

Some tasks, such as `graphene` and `imapclient`, require larger bootstrap
surfaces than simpler tasks. Their quality records and annotation instructions
explicitly flag those risks for human review.

